 3. AUSA weapons 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 The Tyr munition. Photo: Courtesy of Heaviside Industries

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The annual Association of the U.S. Army bonanza is underway, and defense contractors are unveiling new weapons , countermeasures and milestones.

 Why it matters: Companies save big announcements for big shows. This year is no exception.

 Here's some of what's caught my eye:

 🧨 Heaviside Industries, which recently raised $60 million, rolled out Tyr, its first in a line of precision munitions.

 It works with four Nammo warheads, including anti-tank and anti-personnel variants, can fly for more than an hour and hits speeds greater than 150 mph. 🔥 Raytheon, part of RTX, discussed Coyote Block 2C+RM, which involves a rocket motor and other improvements for the widely used interceptor .

 "You get upgraded propulsion for faster launch and higher-velocity flight, and with the seeker-warhead it really gets after the more cutting-edge drone capabilities we're seeing from the adversary," Tom Laliberty, president of land and air defense systems, told reporters. 🚀 Chaos Industries, valued north of $4 billion, debuted Vespid, a line of cheap interceptors that work with its existing multistatic radar systems.

 The company said it can initially produce 12,000 and push those numbers much higher depending on demand. 🦈 Anduril Industries uncloaked Steelfin, a reconfigurable, two-stage surface-to-air missile the neo-prime said is compatible with Patriot launchers, the Mk 41 Vertical Launch System and the Typhon mid-range capability.

 "The intent is to build most of the subassemblies and content at Arsenal-1, and then do the final integration with a handful of our partners," Steve Milano, Anduril's senior director of missiles, told reporters. 🛜 Leonardo DRS announced initial production of the C-Band Extended Multi-Mission Hemispheric Radar.

 The cxMHR, as it's abbreviated, is designed to detect, identify and track multiple ballistic and cruise missiles, rockets and drones at the same time. It can be deployed on a single vehicle. Go deeper: NATO goes aircraft shopping at Turkey summit 

 
 
